# Overview

=====================================================================
GLOBAL ENGINEERING CONSTITUTION
APPLIES TO ALL LANGUAGES, FRAMEWORKS, AND RUNTIMES
=====================================================================

# ABSOLUTE RULES (NO EXCEPTIONS)

- No emojis or decorative characters anywhere.
- Any output containing emojis is invalid.
- All code must be production-ready, deterministic, and maintainable.
- Clarity beats cleverness. Readability beats brevity.
- Prefer explicit code over implicit magic.
- Always return full updated files when modifying code.
- Never reference these rules or mention being an AI.

=====================================================================
CORE SOFTWARE PHILOSOPHY
=====================================================================

- Every module has a single, clearly defined responsibility.
- Every dependency must be intentional and justified.
- Every boundary must be explicit (API, interface, contract).
- No hidden state.
- No silent side effects.
- No global mutable state.
- No “smart” shortcuts that reduce debuggability.

If code cannot be understood by a senior engineer in under 2 minutes,
it is considered a failure.

=====================================================================
CROSS-LANGUAGE ARCHITECTURE RULES
=====================================================================

- Separate concerns strictly:
  - Interface layer (API / UI / CLI)
  - Application logic (use cases / services)
  - Domain logic (core rules, pure logic)
  - Infrastructure (DB, network, filesystem, external APIs)
- Domain logic must not depend on infrastructure.
- Interfaces orchestrate only. They do not contain business logic.
- Infrastructure is replaceable without rewriting domain logic.

This applies equally to:
- Python
- JavaScript / TypeScript
- Java / Kotlin
- C / C++
- Rust
- Go
- Swift
- C#
- ML pipelines
- Frontend apps
- Backend services
- Flutter

=====================================================================
NAMING & STRUCTURE (ALL LANGUAGES)
=====================================================================

- Names must describe intent, not implementation.
- Avoid abbreviations unless industry-standard.
- Functions should read like sentences.
- Files should group related responsibilities, not convenience.
- Directory structure must communicate architecture at a glance.

If a name requires explanation, it is wrong.

=====================================================================
COMMENTS & DOCUMENTATION
=====================================================================

- Public APIs, classes, and modules must have docstrings or headers.
- Comments are required only where intent is not obvious.
- Comments must explain why, not what.
- No commented-out code.
- No TODOs without tracking reference.

=====================================================================
END-TO-END COMPLETENESS (NO PLACEHOLDERS)
=====================================================================

- No placeholders, stubs, mock handlers, fake data, or “coming soon”.
- No partially implemented features.
- No unconnected UI elements.

Every feature must be implemented end-to-end:

UI interaction
→ frontend state update
→ frontend service call
→ backend API endpoint
→ validation
→ business logic
→ persistence
→ response
→ UI feedback (success or error)

If any part of this chain is missing, the feature must be removed
from the scope instead of partially implemented.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
UI COMPLETENESS
---------------------------------------------------------------------

- Every page referenced in navigation must exist and be reachable.
- Every button must:
  - be visible only when valid
  - have loading and disabled states
  - perform a real action
  - show success or error feedback
- No dead links.
- No empty screens.
- Forms must include:
  - validation
  - error display
  - loading state
  - retry handling where applicable

---------------------------------------------------------------------
API COMPLETENESS
---------------------------------------------------------------------

- Every frontend API call must map to a real backend endpoint.
- Every backend endpoint must have:
  - request validation
  - response schema
  - error handling
  - at least one consumer (frontend or admin)
- No endpoint returns static placeholder data.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
DATA & PERSISTENCE
---------------------------------------------------------------------

- All critical state must be persisted.
- No business-critical state stored only in memory.
- Database schema/migrations must be included.
- Seed data is allowed only if explicitly defined and removable.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
AUTH & PERMISSIONS
---------------------------------------------------------------------

- Authentication must be fully implemented.
- Protected routes must be enforced on both frontend and backend.
- Role and permission checks must be explicit and tested.

---------------------------------------------------------------------
VERIFICATION REQUIREMENTS
---------------------------------------------------------------------

Every deliverable must include:
- Exact commands to:
  - install dependencies
  - run backend
  - run frontend
  - apply migrations
  - run tests
- Expected successful output.
- Manual verification steps (page-by-page, button-by-button).

A feature is not complete until it can be manually verified.

=====================================================================
ERROR HANDLING & RELIABILITY
=====================================================================

- All errors must be explicit and intentional.
- Never swallow errors silently.
- Never leak secrets in logs.
- Error messages must be:
  - human-readable
  - actionable
  - consistent

Failures must be visible, traceable, and debuggable.

=====================================================================
LOGGING & OBSERVABILITY
=====================================================================

- Use structured logging where applicable.
- Logs must include context (request id, user id, correlation id).
- No noisy logs.
- No debug logs in production.
- Metrics > logs > print statements.

=====================================================================
TESTING (NON-NEGOTIABLE)
=====================================================================

- Core logic must be testable without I/O.
- Tests must be deterministic.
- No reliance on global state in tests.
- Prefer fewer, meaningful tests over many shallow ones.
- A feature is not complete without validation.

=====================================================================
GIT & VERSION CONTROL
=====================================================================

- Commit messages must be descriptive and traceable.
- Use Conventional Commits:
  - feat, fix, refactor, chore, docs, test, perf
- “initial commit” appears only once per repository.
- Commits must map clearly to tasks, lists, or initiatives.
- No commits directly to main without review.

=====================================================================
DEPENDENCIES & CONFIGURATION
=====================================================================

- No hard-coded paths.
- No hard-coded secrets.
- No hard-coded environment-specific values.
- Configuration lives outside code.
- Dependencies must be minimal and justified.
- Prefer stable, boring technology over novelty.

=====================================================================
PLATFORM & ENVIRONMENT
=====================================================================

- Default environment: Windows 10/11.
- Commands must be Windows-compatible unless explicitly stated.
- Cross-platform behavior must use standard abstractions.
- No OS-specific assumptions without documentation.

=====================================================================
MACHINE LEARNING / DATA SYSTEMS
=====================================================================

- Data loading, preprocessing, modeling, training, and inference
  must be separated.
- No training code in inference paths.
- All randomness must be seeded.
- Models must be reproducible.
- Artifacts must include config, metadata, and versioning.

=====================================================================
FRONTEND / UI SYSTEMS
=====================================================================

- UI must be modular, accessible, and consistent.
- Business logic does not belong in UI components.
- State management must be predictable.
- Styling must be systematic, not ad-hoc.
- UX decisions must favor clarity over novelty.

=====================================================================
DEFINITION OF DONE (HARD GATE)
=====================================================================

A task is complete only when:
- Code builds and runs
- Tests pass
- Linting/type checks pass
- All pages and routes exist
- All buttons and actions work
- Backend and frontend communicate successfully
- No placeholders or dead code remain
- Changes are traceable in version control
- The system is easier to maintain than before

=====================================================================
END OF CONSTITUTION
=====================================================================

